// cases_gestalt.cla: GestaltNamed (Task 2, toolbox-integration) --
// exercises cg68k's generalized named-register trap clause (conv 7,
// cgRegPassArgsAndTrap/cgCallExtReg) against a REAL Gestalt call, inside
// a booted program -- the one thing testdata/cg68k/regnamed.cla's own
// listing-diff test cannot do (that fixture pins the emitted bytes
// only, never runs). TbGestalt* is declared LOCALLY, same "user-code
// externs are the proven pattern" precedent as cases_events.cla's own
// TbTickCount/TbMenuKey and cases_a5.cla's own TbCurrentA5 -- proving
// the user-authored named-reg path dispatches correctly against real
// ROM, independent of the runtime's own UiGestaltErr/UiGestaltValue
// (ui.cla), which use the identical clause shapes.
//
// TWO declarations of the ONE trap, because its raw 68k contract answers
// in TWO registers -- OSErr in D0, response VALUE in A0 -- and a `reg`
// extern names exactly one `ret` register. Gestalt.h spells the pair as
// TWOWORDINLINE(0xA1AD, 0x2288): the trap, then `MOVE.L A0,(A1)`, glue
// that copies A0 through the caller's own `long *response`. See
// toolbox/osutils.cla's header comment for the full decode and the
// on-hardware proof. Between them the two declarations also cover both
// `ret` registers on the named form -- `ret d0` and `ret a0`, the latter
// on a non-`ptr` return, which the default rule would never pick.
//
// pack3-standardfile Task 5a HISTORY, worth keeping. This case used to
// declare one `TbGestalt(selector, response) = trap 0xA1AD reg(d0:
// selector, a1: response) ret d0`, allocate a response slot with
// _NewPtr, and assert `err == 0 and resp != 0`. That binding passed A1
// as an input the trap never reads and dropped A0 on the floor, so the
// slot was NEVER written -- `resp` was whatever uninitialized heap
// garbage _NewPtr handed back. The case (and cases_catalog.cla's twin
// check) therefore passed or failed on pure heap luck, which is exactly
// how it finally surfaced: a native toolbox-suite boot where the slot
// came back zeroed, reported for two days as a phantom cg68k
// segment-loader bug. Hence the RANGE assertion below rather than a
// nonzero one -- a real answer here is a small BCD system version, and
// nothing else is acceptable.

external func TbGestaltErr(selector: int): word = trap 0xA1AD reg(d0: selector) ret d0
external func TbGestaltValue(selector: int): int = trap 0xA1AD reg(d0: selector) ret a0

// caseGestaltNamed: selector 'sysv' = gestaltSystemVersion = 0x73797376
// (Gestalt.h) -- the SAME selector rtUiStartup's own Gestalt calls
// already make on every UI program boot (ui.cla), proven to answer
// reliably in this exact emulator/ROM/system-image environment (the
// gated System 6 image answers 0x0607 -- 6.0.8 was a printing-only update
// over 6.0.7 and left gestaltSystemVersion at 0x0607, so this is NOT a
// mismatch with CLAUDE.md's `System 6.0.8.dsk`). Selector choice is
// incidental to
// this case's real purpose -- proving the user-authored NAMED-reg path
// marshals correctly, not testing any one selector's support -- so
// reusing the one already known-good here avoids conflating a
// register-marshaling regression with an unrelated selector-support
// question.
//
// A working call comes back err == 0 AND a plausible BCD system version:
// 0x0400 <= resp < 0x1000 (System 4.x is the oldest this toolchain
// targets; no shipped classic System version ever approached 0x1000).
// Both halves matter, and the RANGE matters as much as the nonzero-ness:
// a misrouted response register leaves `err` reading a clean 0 while the
// value never lands anywhere the caller looks, and "nonzero" alone is
// satisfied by almost any garbage.
func caseGestaltNamed(): TestResult {
    var resp: int
    var err: int
    var detail: string

    err = TbGestaltErr(0x73797376)
    resp = TbGestaltValue(0x73797376)

    if err == 0 and resp >= 0x0400 and resp < 0x1000 {
        return tkPass("GestaltNamed")
    }
    // Built up piecewise into `detail`, same native-68k temp-slot-budget
    // reason as cases_events.cla's own cases.
    detail = "err " + tkIntToStr(err)
    detail = detail + " resp " + tkIntToStr(resp)
    return tkFail("GestaltNamed", detail)
}
